] [17592186506493 #edb/ref 1077 "field-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506493 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186505919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506493 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e218e9f7-9f97-55c2-8470-2d5b2b9d85fc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506501 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186506082 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506501 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506501 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506501 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186506082 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506501 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f5316a49-e71e-5b2a-be34-e3d00be0b5c1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506509 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186506473 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506509 #edb/ref 1069 "q-138850" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506509 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506509 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the function $y=({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.$  The derivative of this function is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{({x}^{2}+2x)^{5}}{3\\sqrt[3]{{sin}^{2}⁡x}}((ax+36)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(b{x}^{2}+cx)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506509 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "06abf62c-6dfa-52f6-818c-3858ac967b34" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506509 #edb/ref 1074 "Our function can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{1/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the product and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{1/3}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}+2x)^{6})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{1/3}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =6\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+2x)+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+2x)+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+2x}\\cdot (2x+2)+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{12(x+1)}{{x}^{2}+2x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{12(x+1)}{{x}^{2}+2x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}(\\frac{12(x+1)}{{x}^{2}+2x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{3}) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}(\\frac{12(x+1)}{{x}^{2}+2x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}(\\frac{36(x+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+({x}^{2}+2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{3({x}^{2}+2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+2x)^{5}}{3\\sqrt[3]{{sin}^{2}⁡x}}((36x+36)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(1{x}^{2}+2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\na+b+c=36+1+2=39.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506513 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186506474 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506513 #edb/ref 1069 "q-138831" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506513 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506513 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the function $y=\\frac{(x+6)^{3}{cos}^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{7}}.$  The derivative of this function is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\frac{(x+6)^{2}{cos}^{3}⁡x}{(x-1)^{8}}((ax+45)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+(4{x}^{2}+bx+c)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506513 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "11c48698-c6fd-5725-a4b0-1737e6fdc309" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506513 #edb/ref 1074 "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{(x+6)^{3}{cos}^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{7}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the product, quotient, and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{(x+6)^{3}{cos}^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{7}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x+6)^{3})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({cos}^{4}⁡x)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x-1)^{7}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+6)+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+6)+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+6}+4\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-7\\cdot \\frac{1}{x-1} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{3}{x+6}-4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\frac{7}{x-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{3}{x+6}-4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\frac{7}{x-1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\frac{(x+6)^{3}{cos}^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{7}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{(x+6)^{3}{cos}^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{7}}(\\frac{3}{x+6}-4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\frac{7}{x-1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+6)^{3}{cos}^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{7}}(\\frac{3(x-1)-4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x(x+6)(x-1)-7(x+6)}{(x+6)(x-1)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+6)^{3}{cos}^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{7}}(\\frac{-4x-45-4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x({x}^{2}+5x-6)}{(x+6)(x-1)}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{(x+6)^{2}{cos}^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{8}}(4x+45+4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x({x}^{2}+5x-6)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{(x+6)^{2}{cos}^{3}⁡x}{(x-1)^{8}}((4x+45)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x({x}^{2}+5x-6)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{(x+6)^{2}{cos}^{3}⁡x}{(x-1)^{8}}((4x+45)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x(4{x}^{2}+20x-24))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\na+b+c=4+20+(-24)=0.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506517 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186506488 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506517 #edb/ref 1069 "q-138800" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506517 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506517 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the function $y=({x}^{4}+1){3}^{x+4}.$ The derivative of this function is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={3}^{x+4}\\cdot (a{x}^{4}+b{x}^{3}+c).\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506517 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "174736f7-0588-55bb-bfa7-155dc6d18a73" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506517 #edb/ref 1074 "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=({x}^{4}+1){3}^{x+4}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{4}+1){3}^{x+4}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{4}+1)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({3}^{x+4}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{4}+1)+(x+4)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{4}+1)+(x+4)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}+1}\\cdot 4{x}^{3}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{x}^{4}+1}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{x}^{4}+1}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=({x}^{4}+1){3}^{x+4}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =({x}^{4}+1){3}^{x+4}(\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{x}^{4}+1}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)) \\\\  & ={3}^{x+4}(4{x}^{3}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)({x}^{4}+1)) \\\\  & ={3}^{x+4}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3){x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\na+b+c=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+4+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)=4+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506518 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186506418 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506518 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343952" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506518 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506518 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $y=\\sqrt[4]{\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{(2{x}^{2}+1)^{8}}},$ find ${y}^{′}.$\n\n| ${y}^{′}=$ | $-$ | $x($\n{{field-1}}$)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $2(2{x}^{2}+1)^{3}\\sqrt[4]{({x}^{2}+4)^{3}}$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506518 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "19b1b664-4a0c-5ebc-8d57-f886d4c9f0c2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506518 #edb/ref 1074 "Our function can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt[4]{\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{(2{x}^{2}+1)^{8}}} \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{(2{x}^{2}+1)^{8}}]}^{1/4} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+4)^{1/4}(2{x}^{2}+1)^{-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the product and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}+4)^{1/4}(2{x}^{2}+1)^{-2}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}+4)^{1/4})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((2{x}^{2}+1)^{-2}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+4)-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+4)-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}+1)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+4}\\cdot (2x)-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}+1}\\cdot (4x) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{x}{2({x}^{2}+4)}-\\frac{8x}{2{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}-\\frac{16x}{2{x}^{2}+1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{y}{2}(\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}-\\frac{16x}{2{x}^{2}+1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\sqrt[4]{\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{(2{x}^{2}+1)^{8}}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\sqrt[4]{\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{(2{x}^{2}+1)^{8}}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}-\\frac{16x}{2{x}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+4)^{1/4}}{2(2{x}^{2}+1)^{2}}(\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}-\\frac{16x}{2{x}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+4)^{1/4}}{2(2{x}^{2}+1)^{2}}(\\frac{x(2{x}^{2}+1)-16x({x}^{2}+4)}{({x}^{2}+4)(2{x}^{2}+1)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+4)^{1/4}}{2(2{x}^{2}+1)^{2}}(\\frac{-14{x}^{3}-63x}{({x}^{2}+4)(2{x}^{2}+1)}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{x(14{x}^{2}+63)}{2(2{x}^{2}+1)^{3}\\sqrt[4]{({x}^{2}+4)^{3}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506521 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186506402 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506521 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343542" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506521 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506521 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $y=({x}^{2}+1)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{x},$ find ${y}^{′}.$\n\n${y}^{′}=(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{x}($\n{{field-1}}$\\,+\\,2x+x({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x))$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506521 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1d911ae7-875c-567e-a470-7c373f982af8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506521 #edb/ref 1074 "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=({x}^{2}+1)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the natural logarithm of both sides and use the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}+1)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\cdot (2x)+(x)^{′}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+x{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x))}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+x\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=({x}^{2}+1)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =({x}^{2}+1)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{x}(\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{x}(2x+({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+x({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cot}⁡x) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{x}(({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+2x+x({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506527 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186506463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506527 #edb/ref 1069 "q-312672" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506527 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506527 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $y=\\sqrt{\\frac{{({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8)}^{2}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}},$ find ${y}^{′}.$\n\n${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}($\n{{field-1}}$+\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡x({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8))$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506527 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "254041d3-aef5-5074-80f4-67b8b1344bc0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506527 #edb/ref 1074 "Our function can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8)}^{2}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8)}^{2}}}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{1/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the quotient and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{1/2}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{1/2}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8}\\cdot (4{x}^{3}+4x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{4{x}^{3}+4x}{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8}+\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}{2} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{8{x}^{3}+8x}{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{y}{2}(\\frac{8{x}^{3}+8x}{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\sqrt{\\frac{{({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8)}^{2}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8)}^{2}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{8{x}^{3}+8x}{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{|{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8|}{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}(\\frac{8{x}^{3}+8x}{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8>0\\text{ }∀x\\in R$, so $|{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8|={x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8$ and we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8}{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}(\\frac{8{x}^{3}+8x}{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}[\\frac{8{x}^{3}+8x}{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8}\\cdot ({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8)+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}(8{x}^{3}+8x+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8))\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506532 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186506432 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506532 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186506453 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506532 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186506482 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506532 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343350" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506532 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506532 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the function $y=\\frac{{6}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{(x-2)^{4}(3-x)^{3}}.$  Find ${y}^{′}.$\n\n| ${y}^{′}$ | $=$ | ${6}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,($\n{{field-1}}$(x-2)(3-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}$x\\,-$\n{{field-3}}$)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $(x-2)^{5}(3-x)^{4}$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506532 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "310e9298-b624-54d9-ac03-bed77b7fdecc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506532 #edb/ref 1074 "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{{6}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{(x-2)^{4}(3-x)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the quotient, product, and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{6}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{(x-2)^{4}(3-x)^{3}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({6}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x-2)^{4}(3-x)^{3}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡6-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x-2)^{4})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((3-x)^{3})) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡6-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡6)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2))-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡6)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4\\cdot \\frac{1}{x-2}-3\\cdot \\frac{-1}{3-x} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡6)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\frac{4}{x-2}+\\frac{3}{3-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡6)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\frac{4}{x-2}+\\frac{3}{3-x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\frac{{6}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{(x-2)^{4}(3-x)^{3}}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{{6}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{(x-2)^{4}(3-x)^{3}}(-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡6)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\frac{4}{x-2}+\\frac{3}{3-x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{6}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{(x-2)^{4}(3-x)^{3}}(\\frac{-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡6)(x-2)(3-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4(3-x)+3(x-2)}{(x-2)(3-x)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{6}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{(x-2)^{4}(3-x)^{3}}(\\frac{-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡6)(x-2)(3-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+7x-18}{(x-2)(3-x)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{6}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡6(x-2)(3-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+7x-18)}{(x-2)^{5}(3-x)^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506543 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186506427 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506543 #edb/ref 1069 "q-312658" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506543 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506543 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $y={x}^{x}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,$ find ${y}^{′}.$\n\n${y}^{′}={x}^{x}($